WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.548
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.848 --> 00:00:09.396
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.696 --> 00:00:16.250
Unit eleven: International Relations Theory. Today you will contrast theories using whereas / in contrast.

4
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.225
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.121
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:24.926
Realism.

7
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:30.993
Realism means the theory that states pursue power and security in an anarchic world.

8
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:34.790
Realism expects rivalry between great powers.

9
00:00:35.383 --> 00:00:37.734
Your turn. Say: Realism.

10
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.450
Realism.

11
00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:43.148
Liberalism.

12
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:48.750
Liberalism means the theory that cooperation and institutions can reduce conflict.

13
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.476
Liberalism points to trade and international law.

14
00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:55.596
Your turn. Say: Liberalism.

15
00:00:58.433 --> 00:00:59.609
Liberalism.

16
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:01.641
Constructivism.

17
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:07.448
Constructivism means the theory that ideas and identities shape world politics.

18
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.257
Constructivism explains how norms change behaviour.

19
00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.746
Your turn. Say: Constructivism.

20
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.949
Constructivism.

21
00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:20.603
Anarchy.

22
00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:25.172
Anarchy means the absence of a central authority above states.

23
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:28.352
Anarchy in this sense does not mean chaos.

24
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.347
Your turn. Say: Anarchy.

25
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.085
Anarchy.

26
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.304
Balance of power.

27
00:01:38.317 --> 00:01:42.487
Balance of power means a distribution of strength that discourages dominance.

28
00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:45.582
A balance of power can deter aggression.

29
00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:49.555
Your turn. Say: Balance of power.

30
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.982
Balance of power.

31
00:01:54.717 --> 00:01:56.124
National interest.

32
00:01:57.117 --> 00:02:02.065
National interest means the goals a state pursues in its own security and welfare.

33
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.472
Leaders often justify policy by national interest.

34
00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:10.061
Your turn. Say: National interest.

35
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.424
National interest.

36
00:02:15.183 --> 00:02:17.264
International institution.

37
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.734
International institution means an organisation through which states cooperate.

38
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.476
The WTO is an international institution.

39
00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:30.200
Your turn. Say: International institution.

40
00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:35.031
International institution.

41
00:02:35.783 --> 00:02:36.901
Sovereignty.

42
00:02:37.917 --> 00:02:40.926
Sovereignty means supreme authority within a territory.

43
00:02:41.517 --> 00:02:44.050
Sovereignty limits outside interference.

44
00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.372
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty.

45
00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:51.374
Sovereignty.

46
00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:56.015
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:01.369
Visal, why do powerful countries keep arming even when they trade peacefully?

48
00:03:02.483 --> 00:03:13.414
Realism emphasizes power, whereas liberalism emphasizes cooperation. Realists see anarchy as forcing states to protect themselves.

49
00:03:14.517 --> 00:03:16.040
And liberals disagree?

50
00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:29.404
In contrast, liberals argue that institutions and trade make war costly. Constructivists, however, stress that identity shapes what states consider a threat.

51
00:03:30.517 --> 00:03:31.645
Which is right?

52
00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:47.128
Each captures part of the picture. Realism explains rivalry, whereas liberalism explains cooperation; in contrast to both, constructivism explains change.

53
00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:50.127
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:50.983 --> 00:03:54.829
Grammar focus: Contrast connectors (whereas, in contrast).

55
00:03:55.129 --> 00:03:56.362
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:02.033
Realism emphasizes power, whereas liberalism emphasizes cooperation.

57
00:04:02.917 --> 00:04:08.448
Realists see anarchy as a threat. In contrast, liberals see institutions as a remedy.

58
00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.882
By contrast, constructivists stress the role of identity.

59
00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:23.013
Whereas joins two clauses (A, whereas B). 'In contrast' and 'by contrast' start a new sentence and are followed by a comma. Both need two parallel ideas.

60
00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.957
Be careful! Don't say: Realism stresses power, whereas liberalism trade.

61
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.465
Say: Realism stresses power, whereas liberalism stresses trade.

62
00:04:35.765 --> 00:04:37.788
Keep a full verb after whereas.

63
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.078
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:41.867 --> 00:04:43.425
Three Lenses on the World.

65
00:04:43.725 --> 00:04:48.499
International relations theory offers competing explanations of why states act as they do.

66
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:56.921
Realism emphasizes power and security, whereas liberalism emphasizes cooperation through trade, law and institutions.

67
00:04:57.933 --> 00:05:04.647
Realists regard anarchy, the absence of a world government, as forcing states to rely on a balance of power.

68
00:05:05.633 --> 00:05:12.439
In contrast, constructivists argue that the national interest is not fixed but shaped by identities and shared ideas.

69
00:05:13.433 --> 00:05:17.964
No single theory explains every event, so analysts often combine them.

70
00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:23.370
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:24.217 --> 00:05:28.991
Realism emphasizes power, blank liberalism emphasizes cooperation.

72
00:05:29.291 --> 00:05:33.432
A, whereas. B, because. C, unless.

73
00:05:36.967 --> 00:05:38.827
The answer is A: whereas.

74
00:05:39.983 --> 00:05:43.237
The absence of a central authority above states is blank.

75
00:05:43.537 --> 00:05:47.573
A, anarchy. B, sovereignty. C, balance.

76
00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:53.040
The answer is A: anarchy.

77
00:05:54.183 --> 00:05:57.541
The theory that ideas and identities shape politics is blank.

78
00:05:57.841 --> 00:06:02.435
A, realism. B, constructivism. C, liberalism.

79
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.139
The answer is B: constructivism.

80
00:06:09.283 --> 00:06:11.039
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:11.339 --> 00:06:19.950
A, In contrast, liberals favour trade.. B, In contrast liberals, favour trade.. C, In contrast, whereas liberals favour trade..

82
00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:27.253
The answer is A: In contrast, liberals favour trade..

83
00:06:28.417 --> 00:06:30.904
According to the reading, analysts often…

84
00:06:31.204 --> 00:06:36.413
A, combine theories. B, ignore theories. C, reject all theories.

85
00:06:39.967 --> 00:06:42.547
The answer is A: combine theories.

86
00:06:43.733 --> 00:06:47.129
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:47.983 --> 00:06:52.200
Which theory of international relations do you find most convincing, and why?

88
00:07:09.017 --> 00:07:10.621
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:10.921 --> 00:07:36.845
I find a combination convincing, though realism explains rivalry well. States do worry about security, and power matters. However, liberalism shows how trade and institutions reduce conflict, whereas constructivism explains why alliances change over time. No single theory captures everything, so I treat them as complementary lenses.

90
00:07:37.145 --> 00:07:39.296
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:40.183 --> 00:07:45.262
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and contrast connectors (whereas, in contrast).

92
00:07:45.562 --> 00:07:50.548
Today's value is global perspective. Local stories can carry universal truths.

93
00:07:50.848 --> 00:07:53.199
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

94
00:07:53.499 --> 00:07:56.662
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

